 "summary": "Relates to the New York state science, technology, engineering and mathematics incentive program.",
 "title": "Relates to the New York state science, technology, engineering and mathematics incentive program",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A1808",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A1808-2017",
     "printNo": "A1808",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 207\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n \nSenate Bill number 2482-C, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  education law, in relation to the New York\n      state masters-in-education teacher incentive scholarship program\"\n \nSenate Bill number 6089, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend chapter 507  of  the  laws  of  1974,  relating  to\n      providing for the apportionment of state monies to certain nonpub-\n      lic  schools,  to  reimburse  them for their expenses in complying\n      with certain state requirements for the  administration  of  state\n      testing  and  evaluation  programs  and for participation in state\n      programs for the reporting of basic educational data, in  relation\n      to  the  calculation  of nonpublic schools' eligibility to receive\n      aid\"\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n \nAssembly Bill number 934-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to  state  appropri-\n      ations to the state university of New York and the city university\n      of New York\"\n \nAssembly Bill number 967, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the state finance law, in relation to five-year\n      capital plans for the state university of New York  and  the  city\n      university of New York\"\n \nAssembly Bill number 1808-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  education law, in relation to the New York\n      state science, technology, engineering and  mathematics  incentive\n      program\"\n \nAssembly Bill number 7697, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  education law, in relation to the enhanced\n      tuition awards\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  New York's education system  and  its  investments  in  education  are\nunparalleled.  The  2017-2018  Budget  increased  Education Aid for K-12\neducation by $1.1 billion. During the past  six  years  of  my  Adminis-\ntration,  the  State's  funding  for  Education  Aid  has increased $6.2\nbillion, or 32 percent. New York State now leads the nation in  spending\nper pupil on K-12 education.\n \n  At  the  same time, my Administration has made significant investments\nin support of higher education institutions and  student  financial  aid\nprograms,  increasing  funding  by  $1.3  billion,  or  22  percent. The\n2017-2018 Budget provides a record $7.5 billion  in  total  support  for\nhigher education, a $448 million, or 6.3 percent increase over the prior\nyear. New York State also provides more funding to independent (private)\nhigher education institutions than any state except one.\n \n  My  commitment  to  funding  education  at  all  levels is unwavering.\nCollectively, however, these bills would add  hundreds  of  millions  of\ndollars in increased and unbudgeted costs to the State's financial plan,\nwhich  will  ultimately  be  shouldered  by  the State's taxpayers. Such\neducation funding decisions, both in terms  of  revenues  available  and\nappropriations  to be expended, have always been, and should continue to\nbe, addressed in the context of the annual  budget  negotiations.  At  a\ntime  when  the Federal government has enacted, and is threatening addi-\ntional, devastating cuts to the State,  it  would  be  irresponsible  to\nincur such unbudgeted costs at this time.\n \n  Moreover,  these  bills  would  violate  existing agreements that were\npreviously negotiated with the Legislature during the budget negotiation\nprocess. There is no reason  to  violate  these  negotiated  agreements,\nespecially  when it would place an unnecessary and significant strain on\nState resources. For these reasons,  I  am  constrained  to  veto  these\nbills.\n \n  These bills are disapproved.                (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
